§§ `The prohibition on a relevant officer's disclosure as a regulative rule (ruling N2)`

-- Reg 74(4) names the bearer (R) of a prohibition.  Ruling N2: the prohibition (SHANT) is derived from the decision function's answer, and the Hohfeld kind is not typed into any answer.  Where the function answers JUST TRUE the prohibition stands; otherwise nothing is prohibited.  A trace prints its outcome, and a residual obligation cannot be compared on the main binary, so the assertions are on the decision function and on the case where nothing is prohibited.
-- src:2380 | (4) R must not disclose to any person other than the FCA (if R is acting pursuant to arrangements made with the FCA), the Commissioners (if R is acting pursuant to arrangements made with the Commissioners), R's authority or, as the case may be, the Department of the Economy, information obtained by R in the exercise of such powers unless—
-- src:2381 | (a) R has the approval of the FCA or where appropriate the Commissioners to do so, or
-- src:2382 | (b) R is under a duty to make the disclosure.
@ref reg 74(4) (SHANT derived from the function) (src:2380-2382)
DECLARE `reg 74(4) party` IS ONE OF
    `reg 74(4) R`

DECLARE `reg 74(4) action` IS ONE OF
    `reg 74(4) R discloses the information`

@ref reg 74(4) (src:2380)
GIVEN `reg 74(4) R must not disclose the information` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `reg 74(4) party` `reg 74(4) action`
DECIDE `reg 74(4) the prohibition on R` IS
    IF `reg 74(4) R must not disclose the information` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `reg 74(4) R`
         SHANT `reg 74(4) R discloses the information`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BY `reg 74(4) R` BECAUSE "R disclosed information obtained in the exercise of powers under Part 8 to a person to whom regulation 74(4) forbids disclosure"
    ELSE FULFILLED
